WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.580
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:24.330
Good morning.

9
00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:25.510
Good morning.

10
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:26.390
Good afternoon.

11
00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:27.270
Good evening.

12
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.410
You're very peppy for such a serious subject.

13
00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:34.510
I am really excited about this episode.

14
00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.370
Everyone, it's murder.

15
00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.190
Oh, dun-dun-dun.

16
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:44.670
Season 6, Episode 10, written by Daniel Chun and directed by Greg Daniels.

17
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.450
And we have a special treat this week for you for our summary.

18
00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:49.430
We do.

19
00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:54.010
Okay, so you guys, you know I have a big Southern family.

20
00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:56.330
We're spread out between Louisiana and Texas.

21
00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.290
And I know it's not Savannah, Georgia.

22
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.030
But my sister, Billie Jo, has a great Southern accent.

23
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.370
And I asked her to read our summary today.

24
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:06.810
And she did.

25
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:07.690
Here it is.

26
00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.570
I do declare there's been a murder.

27
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:18.250
Michael attempts to distract his employees with a murder mystery party after everyone hears rumors that the company might be going bankrupt.

28
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:23.890
Jim wants everyone to keep walking and is frustrated by Michael's game.

29
00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:28.170
Meanwhile, Andy attempts to ask Aaron out on a date.

30
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.750
Angela, she nailed it.

31
00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:31.990
She nailed it.

32
00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.250
Jenna, I was so tickled by this.

33
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.250
I knew I wanted someone in my family.

34
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.910
Because this episode partly takes place in Savannah, Georgia.

35
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:40.350
Yes.

36
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:44.450
So I knew I would want someone from my Southern family to give the summary a go.

37
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:45.530
And thank you, Billie.

38
00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.350
Thank you, Billie, for giving it a shot.

39
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.430
Well, she can do the summary anytime, Ange.

40
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:50.950
Billie Jo, did you hear that?

41
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:53.690
But right now, she's like, no, no, no, no, no.

42
00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:55.170
Somewhere she's yelling, no, no, no.

43
00:01:56.570 --> 00:01:58.530
Well, let's get into fast facts.

44
00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:04.830
Fast fact number one is all about our new writer for this episode, Daniel Chun.

45
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:06.130
We know him as Danny.

46
00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.330
He started on the writing staff for season six.

47
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.170
He was a supervising producer.

48
00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.590
And this was his first script.

49
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:18.450
Danny had previously written on The Simpsons for six years.

50
00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:21.610
I was able to get back in touch with him, which was so fun.

51
00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.870
And he told me that he knew a lot of the office writers socially,

52
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.030
just through the comedy writing community.

53
00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.470
He knew BJ for a long time.

54
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.150
He said that he loved being at The Simpsons,

55
00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:37.790
but he kind of felt like 80% of working on that show was just punching up jokes.

56
00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.350
Unless you were the showrunner.

57
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.090
So he didn't get to do a lot of long-form writing there, I guess.

58
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:45.930
He said he was really hungry for a different challenge.

59
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.870
BJ had always been a big supporter of his writing,

60
00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:54.390
and he showed Mindy and Paul some stuff that Danny had written just for fun on MySpace.

61
00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:55.690
Remember MySpace?

62
00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:56.690
Oh, my God, yeah.

63
00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.730
He said that gives you a sense of how long ago this was.

64
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.410
But the stuff he had written on MySpace got him a meeting.

65
00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:08.130
They brought him in, and that's how he got hired to be one of our writers for season six.

66
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.110
See, guys, you never know.

67
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.430
You never know.

68
00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:16.990
Yeah, all that stuff that you're writing, if it's a blog, if it's a vlog, I don't even know.

69
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:18.330
You're creating it.

70
00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:19.550
All the logs out there.

71
00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:20.190
All of them.

72
00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:21.370
Someone could see it.

73
00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:21.950
Yes.

74
00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:30.590
So, fast fact number two, I was able to talk to Danny a little bit about the inspiration for this episode of murder.

75
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.110
What was it?

76
00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.330
He said this was a card on the wall.

77
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:36.830
What did the card say?

78
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:41.170
The card said, Michael makes everyone play a murder mystery game.

79
00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:45.290
It was one of the pitches they had come up with over the summer.

80
00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:48.590
He also remembered a card that never got made.

81
00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.010
It was this.

82
00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.330
They go on a ski trip, and someone makes a huge stain on the wall.

83
00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:55.650
Ew.

84
00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.690
I know, but he was pointing out, like, this is how simple a card could be.

85
00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:07.470
But they picked this one, and then they had to figure out how, why, what happens?

86
00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.070
How is this a whole episode?

87
00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:14.810
So, on Mom Detectives, a card could be two ladies on a train with a succulent.

88
00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:15.550
Yes!

89
00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:17.230
And that has to be a whole episode.

90
00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:17.910
That's an episode.

91
00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:18.450
Yeah.

92
00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.650
And you have to turn that into something.

93
00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:20.930
Yeah.

94
00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:29.290
Danny said they also, for season six, had all of these mini arcs that they were going to add to the season.

95
00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:33.750
Those are little stories that take place over several episodes.

96
00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:38.110
And one of those ideas was that Dunder Mifflin was going to go bankrupt.

97
00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:42.910
And another one was that Jim would get this co-manager position.

98
00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:43.970
That's already happened.

99
00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:52.250
But this episode of the murder mystery game, they thought it tied in really well with that mini arc of Dunder Mifflin going out of business.

100
00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:53.350
Oh, for sure.

101
00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:56.710
So, there is a DVD commentary for this episode.

102
00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:57.270
Oh!

103
00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:01.410
So, it features Ellie Kemper, Danny Chun, and Greg Daniels.

104
00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:05.150
And they talked a little bit about this episode setting up three major arcs.

105
00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:06.450
Like, exactly what you're saying.

106
00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:12.190
The crumbling of Dunder Mifflin, the sail to saber, and Andy and Aaron.

107
00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:14.110
Yes, the Andy and Aaron.

108
00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:14.590
You're right.

109
00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:15.470
That's another one.

110
00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:24.950
Danny also told me that the writers played one of these murder mystery games for research in the writer's room.

111
00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:29.670
He said they noticed that in all of the games, the people had silly pun names.

112
00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:31.650
So, they knew they were going to have to do that.

113
00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.930
There was a CD with a narrator that would help you along.

114
00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.430
He said in the game they played, his character was a Frenchman.

115
00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:43.050
And so, he was stumbling through a French accent while they were playing.

116
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:47.450
And that kind of gave them the idea that they should have a game where everybody has to have accents.

117
00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:55.010
He also said that they originally thought that this game could be murder in Little Italy.

118
00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.810
But they were afraid that would feel too much like the Mafia episode.

119
00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:03.070
And so, that's when they pitched the game Beset in Savannah.

120
00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:09.290
Well, Danny said mainly he just remembered feeling like the game was not that much fun.

121
00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.130
It involved a lot of acting.

122
00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.710
And so, he thought about that.

123
00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:19.270
And he thought that's why people like Andy and Michael would like the game.

124
00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:25.130
I just really like how much playing this game in the writer's room informed them.

125
00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:26.350
You know?

126
00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:26.910
Yeah.

127
00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:29.390
Because Danny was like, I don't like playing this game.

128
00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:31.890
I don't like doing an accent and it's too much acting.

129
00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:35.550
I feel like he now could write very well for Angela.

130
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:36.630
And Stanley.

131
00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:37.050
Yeah.

132
00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.690
But he's like, somebody would like this.

133
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:40.990
I guess Andy and Michael.

134
00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:43.390
I thought this was an amazing episode.

135
00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:46.510
So, Danny, my gosh, your first one out of the gate.

136
00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:47.290
Home run.

137
00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:48.650
I really liked it too.

138
00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.050
All right.

139
00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:53.990
Fast fact number three, Angela, is a fan question from Megan H.

140
00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:55.950
in Edmonton, Alberta, Canada,

141
00:06:55.950 --> 00:07:02.430
who would like to know if either of us have ever done a murder mystery event like in the show.

142
00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:04.770
Great question.

143
00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.610
I actually have a story.

144
00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:07.550
Jenna, do you have one?

145
00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:08.950
I do too.

146
00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:09.710
Okay.

147
00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:10.890
You go firSt.

148
00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:11.390
Oh, okay.

149
00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:16.570
I was once invited to a dinner party where they said there will be dinner and then we will be playing

150
00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:18.390
a game as a group.

151
00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:20.890
Did they say what the game was?

152
00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:21.610
No.

153
00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:22.130
Oh.

154
00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:27.950
And when I got there at dinner, the host explained the game while we ate our meal.

155
00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:29.650
Guess who the hosts were?

156
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:30.350
Who?

157
00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.210
Oscar Nunez.

158
00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:32.890
What?

159
00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:33.510
Yes.

160
00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.030
And his wife, Ursula.

161
00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:40.350
Oscar hosted you for a murder mystery game night?

162
00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:40.830
Yes.

163
00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:43.170
Was this before this episode or after?

164
00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:44.450
Before this episode.

165
00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:45.770
Oh, my gosh.

166
00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:47.710
So, the game was called Mafia.

167
00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:48.590
Oh, yeah.

168
00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.310
This is really a popular game.

169
00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:50.650
Yes.

170
00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:53.730
This was like all the rage in Los Angeles for a very long time.

171
00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:58.750
I don't know if this went over the whole country, but people were crazy about this game.

172
00:07:58.930 --> 00:07:59.830
Crazy about it.

173
00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:01.150
And they had the rules.

174
00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:03.450
I was actually texting with Ursula yesterday.

175
00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.050
I was like, Ursula, what did you guys call it?

176
00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:06.830
Because in my memory, they called it killer.

177
00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.670
And she goes, no, Angela, it was called Mafia.

178
00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:09.090
Yes.

179
00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:12.170
And so, there were about 10 of us at the dinner party.

180
00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:14.610
And there's a few different ways you can play this.

181
00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:21.590
But the way we played it was, there was one mafia, one killer, a detective, a doctor,

182
00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.290
and a narrator, and then you had the civilians.

183
00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:24.970
Okay.

184
00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:27.330
And the narrator kind of moves it along.

185
00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:30.230
The narrator's who knows who's who, right?

186
00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:30.990
Oh, okay.

187
00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:31.710
Because-

188
00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.050
That seems like not as much fun of a part.

189
00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:35.350
It actually is.

190
00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:37.530
I've been the narrator before, and it's kind of fun.

191
00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:38.030
Oh, okay.

192
00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:38.430
Go ahead.

193
00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:42.390
I never played Mafia, which I kind of can't believe, because I'm a sucker for these games.

194
00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:44.070
I want us to do this.

195
00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.170
Oh, my gosh.

196
00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:46.750
Earwolf Company Party.

197
00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:48.890
We play Mafia.

198
00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.170
Have you guys played it?

199
00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:51.810
Sam, Cassie, have you played Mafia?

200
00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:53.030
No, I haven't.

201
00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:57.290
But I do remember the absolute stranglehold that it had over everybody for a while.

202
00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:57.790
Oh, yeah.

203
00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:00.530
Wordle or Animal Crossing at that time.

204
00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:00.930
Yes.

205
00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:01.610
Everybody was in it.

206
00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:02.550
Everyone was playing it.

207
00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:07.350
So, in Mafia, the game is divided into two phases, day and night.

208
00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:14.650
During the night, everyone closes their eyes, and the narrator awakens each role.

209
00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:20.490
So, the narrator starts with the Mafia person, lets them know who it is, because in each round,

210
00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:21.810
it's someone new, right?

211
00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:27.690
And as the Mafia person, the way you kill someone is you wink at them.

212
00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:29.350
So, you have to be real sneaky.

213
00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:30.850
At least that's how we played it.

214
00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:35.050
And then the doctor can choose to save someone or not.

215
00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:40.330
And the civilians have to try to figure out, because every time they awaken, someone is

216
00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:40.630
dead.

217
00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:41.550
Oh.

218
00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:42.090
Right?

219
00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:44.510
Because the Mafia person kills someone each time.

220
00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.630
So, the civilians begin questioning each other, and there's a detective role.

221
00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:54.910
But I imagine, though, also, the Mafia person and the doctor, they're pretending, everyone's

222
00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:56.150
pretending to be a civilian.

223
00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:56.830
Yes.

224
00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:57.190
Right.

225
00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:57.910
Yes.

226
00:09:58.450 --> 00:09:59.950
And it's so much fun.

227
00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:04.050
And one time, I was the Mafia person, and no one figured me out.

228
00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:04.790
Hmm.

229
00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:06.250
But what does that say?

230
00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:09.050
It says you were very good at pretending to be a civilian.

231
00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:11.550
You're diabolical.

232
00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:12.970
It was really, really fun.

233
00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:18.030
But yes, I played a version of this murder mystery with Oscar in real life.

234
00:10:18.410 --> 00:10:18.890
Wow.

235
00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.090
Before this episode.

236
00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.050
Before this episode.

237
00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:22.170
That's very cool.

238
00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:22.790
I know.

239
00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:23.450
Is that crazy?

240
00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:27.190
I did not have to wear a funny hat or have an accent, I should say.

241
00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.130
I guess you could have chosen to.

242
00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:34.890
Well, it is very funny that you say that, Angela, because I did a mini deep dive on the

243
00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:42.250
murder mystery game genre, and they believe that it began in the first half of the 19th

244
00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.270
century with a party game called Wink Murder.

245
00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:46.010
Oh!

246
00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:46.850
Yeah.

247
00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:52.530
One player is secretly selected as a murderer, and they kill other players by winking at them.

248
00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:53.530
That's what we did.

249
00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:57.650
If you get killed, you have to count to five before you die.

250
00:10:57.830 --> 00:10:58.230
Yes.

251
00:10:58.450 --> 00:10:58.830
Right?

252
00:10:59.030 --> 00:10:59.210
Yes.

253
00:10:59.210 --> 00:10:59.670
Is this it?

254
00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:04.770
Yes, because you can't just give it away, so you have to, like, make a moment of it.

255
00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:06.190
This is so weird.

256
00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:08.590
Is Mafia just based on wink murder?

257
00:11:08.590 --> 00:11:10.250
So far, it's sounding like it.

258
00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:11.930
I will say this.

259
00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:16.470
If you play Mafia with your friends, and you're the murderer, and you're not good at

260
00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:17.410
winking...

261
00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:18.370
Oh, no!

262
00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:20.230
I never thought of that!

263
00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:21.470
Like me.

264
00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:24.090
I think I'm good with one eye.

265
00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.310
You just winked at me great with both eyes.

266
00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:27.030
Did I really?

267
00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:27.750
Yeah, you did fine.

268
00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:28.410
Oh.

269
00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:30.230
Well, I'm sorry.

270
00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.590
I watched you closer this time.

271
00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:33.450
The right eye.

272
00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:37.490
You do regular wink with this eye, and then the other eye, you go...

273
00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:38.550
I know.

274
00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:45.070
So I can wink in normal speed with one eye, but the other one is like, boonk, boonk.

275
00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:45.530
Yeah.

276
00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:46.470
Boonk, boonk.

277
00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:48.290
I can see you concentrating on it.

278
00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:49.070
To try to wink.

279
00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:49.430
Yeah.

280
00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.530
So you've got to figure out what's your good eye.

281
00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.470
Well, I have never played Mafia.

282
00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:57.630
I have never played a murder mystery game.

283
00:11:57.770 --> 00:11:58.770
Yeah, what's your story?

284
00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:00.790
But I was an actor...

285
00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:01.870
What?

286
00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:04.310
...in murder mystery dinner theater.

287
00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:05.190
Come on!

288
00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:06.050
Yep.

289
00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:08.090
Is there any footage of that?

290
00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:09.630
Can I see any of that?

291
00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:14.430
I have a photo of myself in my crushed velvet gown.

292
00:12:14.930 --> 00:12:17.170
I played several parts in several different shows.

293
00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:21.550
This was my very first paid live theater acting gig.

294
00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:25.130
I did it in St. Louis, Missouri at the Lemp Mansion.

295
00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:26.990
A bunch of people would come in.

296
00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:28.530
Wait, was it like an old mansion?

297
00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.350
Like a haunted sort of cool like old mansion?

298
00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:32.170
Yeah.

299
00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:34.370
Yeah, and I even had a ghost encounter there.

300
00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:34.970
Come on.

301
00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:35.850
While I was working there.

302
00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:36.530
I did.

303
00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:37.710
I did.

304
00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:39.090
I saw the Lavender Lady.

305
00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:40.490
Come on.

306
00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:41.230
I did!

307
00:12:41.670 --> 00:12:43.210
What is the Lavender Lady?

308
00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:44.090
I don't know.

309
00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:46.470
I didn't know who the Lavender Lady was,

310
00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:50.590
but I was in what was one of the rooms of the house

311
00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:53.410
that they designated for the actors to change in.

312
00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:53.870
Uh-huh.

313
00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:56.590
And no one was supposed to come in there

314
00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:58.170
because that was our changing room.

315
00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:01.350
And I heard the door open behind me.

316
00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:03.890
And I looked up in the mirror in front of me

317
00:13:03.890 --> 00:13:06.130
and behind me by the door, I saw a woman.

318
00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:08.590
And I said, I'm sorry, you can't be in here.

319
00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:10.610
And when I turned around, there was nothing there.

320
00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:14.670
So I was fired up.

321
00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:18.390
I went to the owner of the place and I said,

322
00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.170
listen, if you're going to give us a place

323
00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:22.650
to change our clothes, you need to make sure it's secure.

324
00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:24.790
A woman walked right into that room.

325
00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.270
And he said, no one was in that area.

326
00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:29.690
And I said, well, explain to me how this woman walked in.

327
00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:31.050
He said, what'd she look like?

328
00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:35.470
And I said, well, she was, I kind of tried to describe her.

329
00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:37.650
I was like, as I was describing her, I realized like,

330
00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:40.910
I was like, she's wearing a big hat and like a dress.

331
00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:43.630
And sort of-

332
00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:45.770
I realized I was describing an old timey lady.

333
00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:48.670
And then his face got all white.

334
00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:51.330
And he was like, oh my gosh, you saw the Lavender Lady.

335
00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:52.690
What?

336
00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:53.370
Yeah.

337
00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:54.770
I'm out at that point.

338
00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:56.770
I'm, bye-bye, bye dinner theater career.

339
00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:57.590
I'm gone.

340
00:13:57.810 --> 00:13:59.150
I have to go entertain folks.

341
00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:00.350
They're on their salad course.

342
00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.770
I've got to get out there and tell them a murder has happened.

343
00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:04.130
Wow.

344
00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:04.850
Yeah.

345
00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:07.490
Oh my gosh, that's creepy.

346
00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:08.190
Yeah.

347
00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:10.510
Wow, you did dinner theater lady.

348
00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:13.530
You did theater while someone was like buttering a baguette.

349
00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:14.750
Well, here's the thing.

350
00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:16.010
We did it between courses.

351
00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:17.530
So we'd bring everybody in.

352
00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:18.450
We'd sit them down.

353
00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:19.490
They're getting their drinks.

354
00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:20.910
We're handing out slips of paper.

355
00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:26.890
It was our job during this mingle to find the people who seemed outgoing enough to play

356
00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:27.930
the major roles.

357
00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:29.570
Because you don't want to give-

358
00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:30.090
Wait, wait.

359
00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:32.130
The people eating dinner play a part?

360
00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:32.770
Oh, yes.

361
00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:34.170
Everybody gets a part.

362
00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:36.050
Everybody has a funny name.

363
00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.270
Someone in the room is the murderer.

364
00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:40.090
Oh, I didn't realize that.

365
00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:45.130
I thought you were performing a murder mystery while people ate dinner and watched you.

366
00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:49.750
No, interactive, improvisation slash scripted.

367
00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:50.910
We had jokes.

368
00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:53.610
We had to keep people kind of on the plot.

369
00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:57.190
But, you know, you'd walk people to their table.

370
00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.110
Waiters giving them a drink.

371
00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:00.750
You're hobnobbing with them in character.

372
00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:04.410
And you find the people that seem like they can play the different roles.

373
00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.950
And then you've got these slips of paper and you hand it to them.

374
00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:11.050
And then later you'd be like, where's Bobby Oylton?

375
00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:12.450
Bobby Oylton?

376
00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:13.070
Oh, yes.

377
00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:14.950
I saw you when you came in.

378
00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:17.930
Bobby, what do you have to say for yourself?

379
00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:21.110
And then he has to read off of his little slip of paper.

380
00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:22.550
You were Michael Scott.

381
00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:24.310
Yes, I sort of was.

382
00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:25.470
You were Caleb Crawdad.

383
00:15:25.530 --> 00:15:27.270
I was sort of Caleb Crawdad.

384
00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:27.590
Yeah.

385
00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:28.710
Wow.

386
00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:29.550
Yeah.

387
00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:32.070
So I did that for two years.

388
00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:34.890
But I got 50 bucks a night.

389
00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:39.950
That was, like, good money for acting, for doing what I loved.

390
00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:44.350
I need mom detectives to go to a murder mystery dinner theater.

391
00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:46.450
Put it on a card, Jenna.

392
00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:49.850
Yeah, we should investigate the Lavender Lady sighting.

393
00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:50.510
Yes!

394
00:15:51.030 --> 00:15:52.510
We'll get to the bottom of it.

395
00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.110
We will, but we'll have to be in character and wear outfits.

396
00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:58.450
Yes, because we'll pretend like we're part of the dinner theater.

397
00:15:58.610 --> 00:15:58.890
We're part of the show.

398
00:15:59.190 --> 00:16:00.790
My God, it writes itself.

399
00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:02.590
Oh, my God.

400
00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.550
If I see this on TV one day, I'm going to be so pissed.

401
00:16:06.550 --> 00:16:07.370
I'm going to kick someone's ass.

402
00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:08.590
If it's not you and I.

403
00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:09.590
I know.

404
00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:10.570
Kicking some butts.

405
00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:11.710
All right.

406
00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:13.350
Let's take a break and call our agents.

407
00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:24.780
We are back.

408
00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:30.040
And today only, Dwight Schrute presents This Year in Martial Arts,

409
00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.660
colon updates, advancements, and et cetera.

410
00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:35.820
Yes, like Jim said, there's always lots of changes

411
00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:39.840
to the 1,000-year-old martial arts traditions.

412
00:16:40.920 --> 00:16:42.360
Dwight's going to start with Phyllis.

413
00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:43.900
I clocked some moves.

414
00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:47.460
Phyllis charges Dwight with what looks to be a two-finger eye jab.

415
00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:48.040
Mm-hmm.

416
00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:51.160
Dwight stops her with an arm block that I guess would have broken her arm,

417
00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:52.460
and then he's going to flip her over.

418
00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:53.440
Yeah.

419
00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:55.400
He tries to engage Kevin.

420
00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:56.480
Kevin's a big no.

421
00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:57.320
Mm-hmm.

422
00:16:57.320 --> 00:17:00.720
And all of this is going to lead to Dwight fighting himself.

423
00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:01.840
Yes.

424
00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:05.600
I'd like to point out in this scene at 39 seconds,

425
00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:08.340
Meredith is wearing the designer sweater vest again.

426
00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:10.020
I clocked that, too.

427
00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:13.340
I mean, I feel like this is some sort of personal trolling of me.

428
00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:16.860
Well, I have a few tidbits about this cold open.

429
00:17:17.080 --> 00:17:17.920
Oh, really?

430
00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:19.220
I have some as well.

431
00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:25.940
Well, I'll start by saying we got a lot of mail of people asking if Dwight's self-fight

432
00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:30.860
was choreographed, and I would like to tell you guys that was all Rainn Wilson.

433
00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:32.660
Bravo, Rainn!

434
00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:33.100
Yes.

435
00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:37.380
He came up with all of those moves based on descriptions in the script.

436
00:17:37.660 --> 00:17:41.420
We did have our stunt coordinator, Eric Sulke, on board for the day,

437
00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:44.340
just to make sure that Rainn didn't actually hurt himself.

438
00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:49.780
Well, in the DVD commentary, Danny shared that the original version of this cold open

439
00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:53.900
ended with Dwight and Michael kicking each other in the groin.

440
00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:54.700
Oh.

441
00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:55.440
Yeah.

442
00:17:55.740 --> 00:18:01.860
But instead, they went with a pitch from Jen Salata, where Dwight fights himself instead.

443
00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:05.880
It still ends with Dwight punching himself in the groin,

444
00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:11.780
but it was originally scripted to be a self-kick in the groin.

445
00:18:12.100 --> 00:18:12.900
It said this.

446
00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:14.800
How do you kick yourself in the groin?

447
00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:16.080
Exactly.

448
00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:16.540
Yeah.

449
00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:18.560
Here's what the script said.

450
00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:23.100
Quote, Dwight snaps his leg up and kicks himself in the balls.

451
00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:28.600
Dwight's face shows equally the pain of the kick and the pride of winning.

452
00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:30.640
That's so great.

453
00:18:31.800 --> 00:18:37.840
On the day, they realized there was no way for Rainn to self-kick himself in the balls,

454
00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:40.140
that it was just sort of physically impossible.

455
00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:48.060
However, Danny said in an interview that Greg was convinced there was a way to do it.

456
00:18:48.200 --> 00:18:57.320
If maybe they could attach a strap to Rainn's leg that would help sort of hoist it up somehow.

457
00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:03.520
He even sketched it out on the back of his script, which is a very Greg move.

458
00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:08.640
Can't you see Greg turning a script over and like sketching out the self-groin kick strap?

459
00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:09.980
Here's how it works.

460
00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:10.780
Here's the mechanics.

461
00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:13.160
I want that sketch and I want it in a frame.

462
00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:13.640
Right?

463
00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:14.920
Where is that sketch?

464
00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:18.920
In the end, obviously, this was not happening.

465
00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:21.360
So they had Rainn punch himself in the groin.

466
00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:32.860
Greg shared that he really enjoyed directing the cold open and the tag for this episode because they were like mini action movies or the closest thing our show got to little action sequences.

467
00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:34.780
And Jenna, Ellie shared something.

468
00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:38.860
She said the cold open was filmed the morning of the Emmys.

469
00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:44.020
Oh, so after Rainn beat himself up, he had to go to the Emmys?

470
00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:45.120
We all did.

471
00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:46.340
Wow.

472
00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:47.360
We worked that day.

473
00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:54.960
We have shared this before that many times we worked right up until an event and we would have to get ready at the set.

474
00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:55.620
Yeah.

475
00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.600
And the Emmys were traditionally on a Monday night.

476
00:19:58.880 --> 00:19:59.220
Yeah.

477
00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:03.420
Which was rough because it would be like the beginning of your work week.

478
00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:06.400
Oh, and you'd be wrecked for the whole rest of the week.

479
00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:07.480
Wow.

480
00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:10.180
We worked this day and then we went to the Emmys.

481
00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:12.360
Oh, one last thing.

482
00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:14.840
I have a Dwight flyer count.

483
00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:15.640
Oh.

484
00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:20.300
Because clearly you need to have multiple flyers for your year-end martial art update.

485
00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:20.920
Mm-hmm.

486
00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:26.780
We have one on each of Jim's glass walls and we have one on the door by accounting.

487
00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:29.580
Three in view during the whole cold open.

488
00:20:29.740 --> 00:20:31.140
I don't know about the rest of the bullpen.

489
00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:32.340
Or the kitchen.

490
00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:33.320
Or the break room.

491
00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:33.920
I know.

492
00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:35.020
I'm sure they're everywhere.

493
00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:39.340
They're not there for the rest of the episode, but there's three visible for the cold open.

494
00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:41.500
Well, now we're going to get into this episode.

495
00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:48.740
It opens with some weird banter between Michael and Andy about sculpting stand-up comedy.

496
00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:52.840
And it's interrupted by Dwight receiving an email.

497
00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:55.380
And Michael's like, you need to be more specific.

498
00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:57.280
I get like eight emails a day.

499
00:20:57.480 --> 00:20:58.100
Eight, Jenna.

500
00:20:58.480 --> 00:20:59.620
He gets eight.

501
00:20:59.920 --> 00:21:05.760
It's really weird that before we started recording today, we were discussing our email inboxes.

502
00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:06.700
Yeah.

503
00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:10.480
So, guys, Sam and Jenna are really tidy about their email inbox.

504
00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:18.160
Like, they get to zero and then they ask me what my inbox said and my inbox says 1,582.

505
00:21:18.820 --> 00:21:22.420
And Sam and I are still processing that and we're –

506
00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:24.580
It gave you like hives for a minute.

507
00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:25.900
Yeah, we're having a hard time.

508
00:21:26.060 --> 00:21:27.620
I don't mean this as an attack.

509
00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:28.080
Uh-oh.

510
00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.520
But the way you live is chaos.

511
00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:34.260
Sam's at zero right now.

512
00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:36.560
I'm at 10 unread emails.

513
00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:42.040
Actually, do you remember in the break, there was a great Jenna moment where you were looking for an email and you couldn't find it.

514
00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:43.660
And Jenna goes, oh, I'm sorry, can you not?

515
00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:44.520
Yeah.

516
00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:47.100
I was like, oh, you can't find the email I sent you?

517
